[[Slovenia]] is situated in [[Central Europe]], touching the [[Alps]] and bordering the [[Adriatic Sea]]. The Alps—including the [[Julian Alps]], the [[Kamnik–Savinja Alps]] and the [[Karawanks|Karawank]] chain, as well as the [[Pohorje]] massif—dominate northern Slovenia along its long border with [[Austria]]. Slovenia's coastline stretches approximately {{convert|47|km|mi|0|abbr=on}} between [[Italy]] and [[Croatia]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-10-15 |title=Mediterranean Sea {{!}} Facts, History, Islands, & Countries {{!}} Britannica |url=https://www.britannica.com/place/Mediterranean-Sea |access-date=2024-10-23 |website=www.britannica.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Netherlands |first=Statistics |title=Regions of Europe |url=https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/news/2018/30/two-thirds-of-summer-holidays-booked-online/regions-of-europe |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240826160323/https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/news/2018/30/two-thirds-of-summer-holidays-booked-online/regions-of-europe |archive-date=26 August 2024 |access-date=2024-10-23 |website=Statistics Netherlands |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gause |first=Rich |title=UCF Research Guides: Countries & Regions: Southern Europe |url=https://guides.ucf.edu/countries/southerneurope#s-lg-box-15193200 |access-date=2024-10-23 |website=guides.ucf.edu |language=en}}</ref>

Four major geographic regions meet in Slovenia: the Alps, the [[Dinaric Alps|Dinarides]], the [[Pannonian Plain]], and the Adriatic Sea. Most of Slovenia is in the [[Black Sea]] [[drainage basin]]. The Alps—including the [[Julian Alps]], the [[Kamnik-Savinja Alps]] and the [[Karawanks|Karawank]] chain, as well as the [[Pohorje]] massif—dominate Northern Slovenia along its border with [[Austria]]. 

On the [[Pannonian plain]] to the east and northeast, toward the [[Croatia]]n and [[Hungary|Hungarian]] borders, the landscape is essentially flat. However, the majority of Slovenian terrain is hilly or mountainous, with around 90% of the territory 200 meters or more above sea level. 

The [[Geometric Centre of the Republic of Slovenia|geometric centre]] is located at [[Geographic coordinate system|coordinates]] {{Coord|46|07|11.8|N|14|48|55.2|E}}.<ref>{{cite web |title=Simboli in sestavine |url=http://www.drustvo-geoss.si/page/sl/geoss/simboli_in_sestavine |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120401225025/http://www.drustvo-geoss.si/page/sl/geoss/simboli_in_sestavine |archive-date=1 April 2012 |access-date=12 February 2012 |publisher=GEOSS Society |language=sl}}</ref> It lies in [[Slivna]] in the Municipality of [[Litija]].<ref>{{cite book |url=http://www.stat.si/letopis/2011/01_11/01-01-11.htm |title=Geografske koordinate skrajnih točk |publisher=Statistical Office of the Republic of Slovenia |language=sl, en |trans-title=Geographical coordinates of the extreme points |access-date=12 February 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130826070602/http://www.stat.si/letopis/2011/01_11/01-01-11.htm |archive-date=26 August 2013 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Slovenia's highest peak, [[Triglav]] ({{cvt|2,864|m|ft|0|disp=or}}), is a national symbol, featured on the national [[Coat of arms of Slovenia|coat of arms]] and [[Flag of Slovenia|flag]]. The country's average altitude [[Above mean sea level|above sea level]] is {{cvt|557|m|ft|0}}.[[File:Mangart_16.jpg|thumb|Mount [[Mangart]], in the [[Julian Alps]], is the third-highest peak in Slovenia, after [[Triglav]] and [[Škrlatica]].]]
The term [[Karst topography]] refers to southwestern Slovenia's [[Karst Plateau]], a [[limestone]] region of underground rivers, gorges, and caves, between Ljubljana and the ocean. On the Pannonian plain to the East and Northeast, toward the Croatian and Hungarian borders, the landscape is essentially flat. However, most of Slovenia is hilly or mountainous, with around 90% of its land surface {{cvt|200|m|ft|0}} or more [[Above mean sea level|above sea level]].

As of 2020, 60.4% of Slovenia ({{convert|12380|km2|sqmi|0|abbr=on|disp=or}}) was forested,<ref name="fao_gfra2020">{{cite report |url=https://doi.org/10.4060/ca9825en |title=Global Forest Resources Assessment 2020: Main report |date=18 November 2020 |publisher=FAO |doi=10.4060/ca9825en |isbn=978-92-5-132974-0 |access-date=25 September 2025 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> ranking it second in Europe by [[List of countries by forest area|percentage of area forested]], after [[Finland]].<ref name="owid_land_cover_forest">{{cite web |date=9 May 2025 |title=Share of land covered by forest |url=https://ourworldindata.org/forest-area |access-date=25 September 2025 |publisher=Our World in Data}}</ref> The areas are covered mostly by [[beech]], [[fir]]-beech and beech-[[oak]] forests and have a relatively high production capacity.<ref>{{cite web |year=2009 |title=Forestation and variety of forests |url=http://www.zgs.gov.si/eng/slovenian-forests/forests-in-slovenia/forestation-and-variety-of-forests/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110410123608/http://www.zgs.gov.si/eng/slovenian-forests/forests-in-slovenia/forestation-and-variety-of-forests/index.html |archive-date=10 April 2011 |access-date=2 February 2011 |publisher=Slovenia Forest Service}}</ref> Remnants of [[Old growth|primeval forests]] are still to be found, the largest in the [[Kočevje]] area. Grassland covers {{cvt|5,593|km2|sqmi|0}} and fields and gardens ({{cvt|954|km2|sqmi|0|disp=or}}). Slovenia has {{cvt|363|km2|sqmi|0}} of orchards and {{cvt|216|km2|sqmi}} of vineyards.{{citation needed|date=July 2024}}

